\begin{abstract}
Atrial fibrillation (AF) raises the risk of stroke, and electrocardiogram (ECG) models that predict its onset are explained only after training, by attribution methods that agree poorly with one another. We asked whether a model whose ECG evidence passes through readable factors predicts incident AF from one sinus-rhythm 12-lead ECG more accurately than a clinical model and a black-box network, and where in the cardiac cycle its evidence lies. We propose the block-term tensor network (BTTN), a survival network trained with the Cox partial likelihood on a frequency by time by lead spectrogram tensor. Each block projects the tensor onto a frequency factor and a lead factor. Age, sex and ECG intervals enter through a separate linear term that bypasses these factors. In MIMIC-IV-ECG, incident AF was defined from hospital diagnoses coded in the International Classification of Diseases (ICD), and all methods were compared under one cross-validation protocol. At one year, BTTN with the clinical term had the highest area under the receiver operating characteristic curve (AUROC) of the methods compared: 0.7776 (95\% confidence interval [0.7664, 0.7888]), against 0.7597 [0.7488, 0.7713] for a clinical model and 0.7350 [0.7226, 0.7469] for a convolutional neural network (CNN). The ECG term alone reached 0.7397 [0.7275, 0.7517], comparable to the CNN. One survival model gave AUROCs of 0.723 to 0.745 at horizons from 120 days to 5 years after the ECG, the shortest covering the 90 days after a 30-day blanking window. In the survival sample, which over-represents patients with AF, observed one-year AF rose from 0.55\% in the lowest risk decile to 11.4\% in the highest. Per unit of signal energy, beat-aligned occlusion changed the ECG term's risk most in the PR window. Within one health system, an ECG term built on readable factors, combined with a separate clinical term, gave the highest discrimination of the methods compared, and external validation is the next requirement.
\end{abstract}

The block-term layer has $K = 12$ blocks. Block $k$ holds a frequency factor $\mathbf{A}_k \in \mathbb{R}^{F \times 3}$ and a lead factor $\mathbf{C}_k \in \mathbb{R}^{L \times 2}$, and it projects the tensor onto them:
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{Z}_k = \mathcal{X} \times_1 \mathbf{A}_k^{\top} \times_3 \mathbf{C}_k^{\top} \in \mathbb{R}^{3 \times T \times 2},
\label{eq:block}
\end{equation}
where $\times_n$ denotes the mode-$n$ product, which multiplies each mode-$n$ fibre of the tensor by the matrix. Each block thus gives six time series, one for each pair of a frequency component and a lead component, and the layer gives 72. The projection reduces the frequency and lead modes to ranks 3 and 2, the mode-$n$ ranks by which a block term is characterised \cite{De_Lathauwer_2008}. The time mode is left to the temporal layer of Section~\ref{sec:temporal}. A frequency factor states which frequency bands its block weighs, and a lead factor states which leads. The two can therefore be read as a spectral profile and a spatial profile of the block.

BTTN is trained with the negative Cox partial log-likelihood \cite{Cox_1972}, as DeepSurv trains a neural network \cite{Katzman_2018}. For a mini-batch with event indicators $\delta_i$, times $t_i$ and log-risks $\eta_i$, the loss is
\begin{equation}
\ell = -\frac{1}{\sum_i \delta_i} \sum_i \delta_i \Big( \eta_i - \log \sum_{j:\, t_j \ge t_i} e^{\eta_j} \Big),
\label{eq:cox}
\end{equation}
with risk sets formed within the batch. The time is the interval from the index ECG to AF onset for cases and to the end of follow-up for controls. Training used Adam with a learning rate of $2 \times 10^{-3}$, weight decay of $10^{-4}$, a cosine schedule over at most 120 epochs and batches of 512. Batches without an event were skipped. Within each training fold, 15\% of the patients were held out for early stopping on the validation AUROC, with a patience of 16 epochs. The prediction for a test fold is the mean log-risk of three networks trained with different seeds.

The clinical term is a linear function of nine risk factors. They are age, sex, and seven automated measurements of the index ECG from MIMIC-IV-ECG. The RR interval and the P, QRS and T axes are used as released. The P-wave duration, QRS duration and QT interval were computed from the released onset and end times of the waves. Age is the anchor age of MIMIC-IV \cite{Johnson_2023}. The factors are standardised with the statistics of the training fold, and a missing value is replaced by the training mean. The term $\mathbf{w}^{\top}\mathbf{c}$ has no intercept and is added to the log-risk of the ECG term:
\begin{equation}
\eta = \eta_{\mathrm{ECG}}(\mathcal{X}) + \mathbf{w}^{\top}\mathbf{c}.
\label{eq:fused}
\end{equation}
The weights $\mathbf{w}$ are initialised from a class-balanced logistic regression on the training split. They are excluded from weight decay, so that training does not shrink them towards zero. The PR interval is not among the factors. We call BTTN with the clinical term the fused model.

