    \caption{Response of the ECG-only BTTN to beat-aligned occlusion, as the mean over 120 ECGs. The model was trained on the sample of the survival design. The ECGs are those of patients with incident AF to which the model gave the highest risk, passing over ECGs that could not be read or had fewer than three detected beats. Each panel shows one lead. The black trace is the mean of the ECGs' median beats, with each lead scaled to a maximum absolute amplitude of 1; the vertical scale is not shown. Time is relative to the R peak detected in lead II (dotted line). Windows of 25~ms are centred every 25~ms from 350~ms before to 425~ms after the R peak. For each window, the window was set to zero in every beat of one lead, the spectrogram was rebuilt and the drop in predicted risk was recorded. Shading depth shows the mean drop, with a negative mean set to zero, divided by the mean signal energy removed plus one tenth of that energy averaged over all leads and windows. Depth is relative to the largest value in the figure, and windows below 3 percent of it are not shaded. The P, PR, QRS, ST and T windows of the text group the windows centred 225 to 150~ms before the R peak, 125 to 75~ms before, 50~ms before to 25~ms after, 50 to 125~ms after, and 150 to 375~ms after. Table~\ref{tab:occlusion} gives the span of the beat that each group occludes. The shading describes the response of the fitted model to occlusion and is not an effect on the risk of AF.}
